 /*
- * Max-inflight cap (live-defect fix, 2026-07-14; see the catch-up-drain addendum in
- * docs/design/2026-07-13-vt-sync-output.md in the Skylight tree for the full investigation,
- * including why an active-drain approach was tried, tested, and retracted).
- *
- * sync_deadline_us bounds how long a single pop may block, but this compat-shim calling
- * convention (FF_CODEC_ENCODE_CB) pops at most one completed packet per real
- * avcodec_send_frame() call -- so once a deadline miss lets backlog (submitted-minus-delivered)
- * grow, it is a structural ratchet: submit-rate == pop-rate == 1/cycle forever, even after VT
- * recovers, because nothing about wait mode changes pop *frequency*. Left unchecked this grows
- * without bound under sustained overload (confirmed live: reached 10 frames / ~167ms and never
- * recovered for the rest of the session).
- *
- * IMPORTANT, empirically confirmed (2026-07-14): once backlog exists, NOTHING achievable through
- * this calling convention can shrink it back to zero short of a real flush (avcodec_send_frame
- * (NULL), which is not viable mid-session -- it permanently sets avci->draining and refuses all
- * further frames). A "submit an extra duplicate frame purely to buy another pop" approach was
- * implemented and tested at the Skylight layer; it produced byte-identical inflight trajectories
- * to doing nothing, because every successful pop requires its own paired send_frame call, so the
- * extra pop a burst tick buys is exactly offset by the extra submission that same tick required.
- * Backlog, once nonzero, is therefore a PERMANENT plateau for the life of the encode session --
- * the only lever available is bounding how high that plateau can ever get.
- *
- * That's what this cap does: once inflight (frame_ct_in - frame_ct_out) reaches
- * VT_SYNC_MAX_INFLIGHT, vtenc_q_pop() reverts to the pre-deadline unbounded wait for that call
- * (ignoring whatever deadline_us the caller passed). Because frame_ct_in is incremented for the
- * frame being submitted THIS call before this check runs, the count here always includes that
- * frame's own not-yet-delivered packet -- so the cap's effective steady-state plateau, once
- * backlog has formed, settles at VT_SYNC_MAX_INFLIGHT - 1 pre-existing misses (verified live at
- * VT_SYNC_MAX_INFLIGHT=3: plateaus at 2, not 3). During genuine sustained overload (q_head
- * legitimately empty because VT hasn't finished ANY frame in a while) the forced unbounded wait
- * DOES block for real, providing real backpressure that paces the caller down toward VT's actual
- * throughput (capture drops frames upstream via its own mailbox instead of this queue growing
- * further) -- confirmed live via call-latency spikes during the loaded window. Once any residual
- * backlog exists AND VT is keeping up again, further unbounded waits do not block (q_head is
- * never empty), so the residual neither grows past the cap nor shrinks below it -- it just sits
- * at the plateau, bounded, for the rest of the session. Net effect: permanent added latency is
- * bounded to (VT_SYNC_MAX_INFLIGHT - 1) frame periods (~33ms at 60fps for the value below)
- * instead of the previously-unbounded growth.
- *
- * No new AVOption: this derives entirely from the caller-passed deadline_us being positive
- * (which itself only happens when sync_output=1 and sync_deadline_us>0 are both active for a
- * live frame -- see vtenc_frame()), so the cap is automatically inert whenever the deadline
- * feature itself is inert (deadline_us<=0), matching the "derive, don't add a knob" ask.
+ * Bound a synchronous pop when VideoToolbox is overloaded. The native
+ * receive-packet callback below also permits a later call to drain completed
+ * output without submitting another frame, so Skylight can recover backlog
+ * between capture events rather than retaining it for the whole session.
  */
 #define VT_SYNC_MAX_INFLIGHT 3
 
